WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:10.340
A girl lies sleeping with electrodes
attached to her body.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.740
A man and a woman are looking at her

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.260
through a one-way glass window
from the next room.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.780
“Waste of time and money,” says the man.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.740
“An illiterate kid from a stone quarry?

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.780
What could she possibly
contribute to the project?”

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.220
“Hardly a kid.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.940
She’s at least 16,” says the woman.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.100
“Come on, we’re making history.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.900
First ever experiment
in collective dreaming.”

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.260
To her right is a screen
that takes up an entire wall.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.860
The 311 participants 
in the experiment are now all asleep.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.940
Her poised finger jabs the keyboard.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.180
The girl’s dream.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.820
Shards of memory.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.380
She’s watching her father at work.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.380
The wooden skeleton of the great loom,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.420
the finished tapestries
hanging on the wall.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.780
Clackety-clack goes the loom.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.020
The river floods,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.140
sweeping her parents away.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.980
The loom comes apart:

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.100
colored threads in the water.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.780
The sound of hammer striking stone.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.620
The choking dust.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.500
The quarry at night.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.140
Young people around the fire.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.340
She lifts a bubbling pot of rice
from the flames.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.820
Someone douses the fire,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:46.540
and she lowers the pot into an insulator
they made out of clay and straw.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.740
The rice cooks with its own heat.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.220
The taste of hot rice and spiced oil.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.860
But look,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.220
there are strangers in her dream.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.980
So many unfamiliar, bewildered faces.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.020
And beside her,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:11.020
a boy, about 12, holding
in his arms a large clock.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.100
“Sync successful.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.060
Activating stage two.”

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:25.420
Suddenly, the 311 dreaming people
are high up in the air.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.580
The Earth dawns below them

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.180
swathed in green and blue,

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.220
punctuated by clouds and storm formations.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:38.420
The most beautiful tapestry
the girl has ever seen.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.700
“Father,” she whispers,

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.500
although he's been dead two years.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.220
“Father, the world itself is a weave,

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.700
a living garment.”

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.900
Slowly, they fall through the air

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.780
and then they see ...

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.700
a vast swath of forest
veined and spotted with fire,

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.340
a gray wall of water
crashing down on a bleak shore,

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.220
drowning islands
ringed with plastic refuse,

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:12.380
beached carcasses of dead whales,

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:17.820
ant-like figures in their millions
walking away from desertified landscapes:

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.060
ruined fields,

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.940
destroyed forests.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:29.100
The grief that envelops the girl
is so enormous a thing

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:35.060
that she can only take it in
through small, gasping breaths of pain.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.540
She alights with the others
on a featureless plain

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.300
under a vast, brooding sky.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.380
There’s the boy
with the clock in his arms.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.940
The thumping of the girl’s heart
keeps time with the boy’s clock,

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.140
an urgent, rapid beat.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.940
Tuck, tuck, tuck, tuck,
tick-tock, tick-tock.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.460
Something’s coming.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.740
“Warning: this is not in the program.”

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.020
Bearing down upon them is a whirlwind.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.860
A chimera of storm clouds
and plastic rubbish and burning trees.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:20.540
A monster made of hot dust
and angry water.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.020
People scream and run,

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.940
but the boy with the clock
is standing still, watching.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.340
Tick-tock, tick-tock, tick-tock.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.700
“Why aren’t you running?” asks the girl.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.980
“I’m studying the unraveling
of the world,” says the boy, earnestly.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.060
“That’s what this monster
represents in our dream.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.140
I’m an engineer and a knowledge-seeker.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.580
Pleased to meet you.”

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.340
She turns away.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.900
There’s a storm inside her:

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.700
grief and memory and anger.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.740
She takes a deep breath

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.900
and walks toward the monster.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.580
She stands before it.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.900
A thin, dark girl
in a tattered, green sari.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:11.340
Her hair is whipped loose from its braid,

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.980
and the end of her sari
unfurls like a flag.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.380
“Stand back,” shouts the boy in alarm.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.940
“Let me figure out the control mechanism.”

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.980
Bending her head,

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.220
straining against the wind,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.980
the girl walks into the monster.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:37.300
The monster has a middle
with a quiet place.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.500
She sits on the bare ground

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.740
and looks up at the towering
vortex around her.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.900
A rain of debris patters down.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.660
There is a deep rumble,

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.820
and she thinks she hears the monster say,

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:53.820
“Why?”

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.660
“To learn,” says the girl.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.180
“I’ve lost everything --

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:00.180
lost my chance to learn at school.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:01.540
Teach me.”

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:08.140
To the boy outside
with the clock in his arms,

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.500
the girl has been consumed by the monster.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.940
In the lab, the man and woman
look through the window

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:17.860
at the girl’s twitching body.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.340
Alarms sound from the instruments
monitoring her vital signs.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:29.780
At last, the girl emerges
from the monster.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.260
The monster is retreating
over the horizon.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.380
The plain is littered with debris.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.700
The girl has cuts on her face and arms.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.460
She walks up to the boy
and speaks to him urgently.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.940
Then they turn
and face the waiting people.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.700
“My teacher taught me,” says the girl,

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.420
“that the world is a living tapestry.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:57.860
The wind and the waves are woven
together with the forests and grasslands,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.980
threaded with the deserts
and snowy regions,

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:05.460
and all living things
are knit together in mutuality.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.580
Although each place has its own pattern,

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.540
the tapestry is one.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.740
As the weave of life
is torn apart in one place,

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.140
the threads unravel in another.”

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:22.620
She and the boy confer again.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.660
“See my clock,” says the boy,
urgently to the crowd.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.740
“It has components
with clearly defined functions

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.620
so you can adjust and control.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.820
Treat each part separately.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.660
You can tinker with it from the outside.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.340
The clock was my orienting
metaphor for the world.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:48.700
But the world is not like a clock, is it?”

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.580
He sets his clock on the ground.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.660
Tick-tock, tick-tock, tick-tock.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.500
“Come on,” says the girl
to the watching people.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.300
She and the boy start putting
the scattered debris together,

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.980
and some of the others join them.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.300
A structure starts to emerge.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.780
An enormous loom of strange design.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.900
The girl tosses
her insulating pot into the loom,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.700
and it turns into a thick spool
of multicolored yarn.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.060
There are levers for feet and hands,

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.900
and with a creak and a groan,

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:25.940
the loom begins to work.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.100
The fabric unfolds:

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.260
a mesmerizing tapestry
of shifting colors and patterns.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:37.260
“What should I do with my clock?”
asks the boy, picking it up.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.220
Tick-tock, tick-tock, tick-tock.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.700
But now the sound
of the loom is getting louder.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.300
Dahganah, denah, dahganah,

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.220
dahganah, denah, dahganah, goes the loom.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.580
“Throw it in!”

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.660
So the boy picks up the clock

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.820
and tosses it into the loom.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.060
When it hits the tapestry,

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:01.260
it breaks up.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:05.140
A fountain of silver glitter
scatters over the weaving.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:12.220
Look, each bit of glitter has turned
into a tiny clock woven with the fabric.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.540
The frantic ticking is slowing down

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.140
to accommodate the rhythm of the loom.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.020
“We’re reweaving
the world!” shouts the girl,

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:26.620
and the dreamers find
themselves within the tapestry --

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.540
not outside, but in it.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.500
Even the man and woman in the lab,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.380
even you and me here in this room,

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:38.060
being woven back
into the fabric of the world.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.180
Tick-tock, tick-tock,

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:41.300
tick-tock,

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:42.380
tick-tock.

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.380
Dahganah, denah, dahganah,

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:46.620
dahganah, denah, dahganah.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:50.060
Thank you.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:56.940
(Applause and cheers)

